UPDATE moments SET title = 'Israel and Avis Blodgett and the Underground Railroad', body = 'The 1846 house at the museum campus belonged to Israel and Avis Blodgett, abolitionists whose home offered shelter and help to people escaping slavery. Its history connects an ordinary domestic building in Downers Grove with a much wider struggle over freedom, law and human rights. The National Park Service identifies the house as a station on the Underground Railroad, with the family''s activity likely extending from the early 1840s until Israel''s death in 1861.

The Blodgetts came from Massachusetts and established a farm and blacksmithing business in the area. Their house replaced an earlier log cabin. Its simple Greek Revival form belongs to the period when settlers were building more permanent homes, but architecture alone does not explain its significance. The important history also concerns what the family did inside the house and the assistance they provided beyond it.

The National Park Service cites first-hand accounts from the Blodgetts'' children and a daughter-in-law, as well as a contemporary antislavery newspaper. Those sources describe aid and shelter at the family home and secret nighttime transport toward the home of Philo Carpenter in Chicago. The house was accepted into the National Underground Railroad Network to Freedom in 2023. This is a documented association, rather than simply a local tradition awaiting its first supporting evidence.

The house has also had a history of relocation and preservation. It was moved when Charles Blodgett built a later family home in 1892, and it returned to the museum campus in 2008 after a preservation campaign. The building''s survival required decisions made long after the events for which it is remembered. Visiting it today means encountering both a nineteenth-century home and the result of a modern effort to preserve evidence of that past.', hero_media_id = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-00000000f0f4', updated_at = now() WHERE id = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-00000000d0d5';

